 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Two pronouns to start with: tôi and bạn",
     "texto": [
      "In Vietnamese, «I» and «you» depend on age, gender and how close you are. There are many, and we will learn them little by little (in lesson 2 **anh, chị, em, cô** come up…).",
      "**tôi** = I. Neutral and polite: used in formal situations or with strangers.",
      "**bạn** = you (and also «friend»). In speech it is not used so much, but it is the neutral «you» of texts and notices. In this book it is used a lot for clarity."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Yes-or-no questions with «không»",
     "texto": [
      "To ask a yes-or-no question, put **không** at the end: **Bạn khỏe không?** = Are you well?",
      "Structure: subject + (**có**) + verb or adjective + **không**. The **có** is almost always left out: «Bạn (có) hiểu không?» = Do you understand?",
      "With adjectives (khỏe, vui…) you don't need a verb like «am» or «is»: **Tôi khỏe** = I'm well.",
      "**không** before a verb or adjective makes it negative: **Tôi không khỏe** = I'm not well; **Tôi không hiểu** = I don't understand.",
      "People usually answer by repeating the verb or adjective («Khỏe.», «Hiểu.») rather than just saying «yes»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
